1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,047
Dana is in her forties, and she has a
chunk of savings she has promised herself

2
00:00:05,047 --> 00:00:06,900
she won't touch for 20 years.

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:12,305
Right now it sits in 3-month Treasury
bills, and every time one comes due, the

4
00:00:12,305 --> 00:00:13,820
money rolls into the next.

5
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:19,645
Lots of people have money like this, set
aside for much later and parked where it

6
00:00:19,645 --> 00:00:21,780
earns whatever short-term rates pay.

7
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:26,216
Tonight she has a different tab open: a US

8
00:00:26,216 --> 00:00:29,706
savings bond with a much simpler promise,

9
00:00:29,706 --> 00:00:35,580
that a bond held for 20 years is
worth at least double what was paid.

10
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:41,200
So which path would have left her
better off, the promise or the roll?

11
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:47,748
One catch before we look back: the
doubling promise in its current form only

12
00:00:47,748 --> 00:00:53,640
began with bonds issued in May 2005, and
earlier bonds came with different terms.

13
00:00:54,580 --> 00:01:00,043
So for every year before that, we simply
pretend today's promise already existed,

14
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:03,280
and you'll see that marked
each time it applies.

15
00:01:04,457 --> 00:01:09,674
By the end, you'll know how often history
let the roll beat double, when it fell

16
00:01:09,674 --> 00:01:14,566
short, and roughly where the line sits
that your own 20 years would have to

17
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:15,566
clear.

18
00:01:16,237 --> 00:01:17,477
First, the bond.

19
00:01:18,277 --> 00:01:22,037
It's a Series EE savings bond,
sold by the US Treasury itself.

20
00:01:22,877 --> 00:01:29,121
For bonds issued May to October 2026, it
earns a fixed 2.40 percent a year, and the

21
00:01:29,121 --> 00:01:32,957
rate for new bonds is reset
every May and November.

22
00:01:32,957 --> 00:01:38,497
That rate alone would not double the money
in 20 years, so at the 20-year mark the

23
00:01:38,497 --> 00:01:41,537
Treasury tops the bond
up to at least double.

24
00:01:42,297 --> 00:01:47,659
The promise, as written, covers bonds held
electronically, which is how the Treasury

25
00:01:47,659 --> 00:01:48,659
sells them now.

26
00:01:49,257 --> 00:01:54,783
Spread over those 20 years, that doubling
can also be written as one steady yearly

27
00:01:54,783 --> 00:01:56,737
rate, shown here on the gate.

28
00:01:56,737 --> 00:02:03,271
And the double only arrives at 20 years:
a bond cashed earlier earns just its fixed

29
00:02:03,271 --> 00:02:09,097
rate, and one cashed before five years
also gives up 3 months of interest.

30
00:02:10,249 --> 00:02:15,109
The Treasury bill path works the other
way around: it makes no promise past three

31
00:02:15,109 --> 00:02:16,109
months.

32
00:02:16,529 --> 00:02:20,650
A bill is a short loan to the government:
the buyer pays a bit less than the face

33
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:23,449
amount and gets the full
amount back when it comes due.

34
00:02:24,409 --> 00:02:29,136
Over 20 years the money rolls through
80 bills, each paying whatever rate the

35
00:02:29,136 --> 00:02:30,609
market sets at the time.

36
00:02:31,589 --> 00:02:36,949
In August 2026, the 3-month
bill paid 3.72 percent.

37
00:02:36,949 --> 00:02:41,729
It's tempting to set that next to the
bond's doubling rate and call it settled.

38
00:02:42,589 --> 00:02:46,819
But these rates aren't measured the same
way, and bill rates are quoted on a scale

39
00:02:46,819 --> 00:02:49,089
where the doubling line
sits a little lower.

40
00:02:49,929 --> 00:02:55,577
The bigger problem is time: that rate
covers one bill, and the roll lives on 80

41
00:02:55,577 --> 00:02:56,577
of them.

42
00:02:57,029 --> 00:03:03,192
What decides the race is the bill rate
across all 20 years, and that is exactly

43
00:03:03,192 --> 00:03:06,469
what nobody knows in advance, us included.

44
00:03:07,329 --> 00:03:10,349
So instead of guessing, we
went back and replayed history.

45
00:03:11,497 --> 00:03:15,242
We took the actual 3-month bill rate for

46
00:03:15,242 --> 00:03:18,613
every month since 1934 and started a

47
00:03:18,613 --> 00:03:22,264
20-year roll in each month from January

48
00:03:22,264 --> 00:03:28,537
1934 to September 2006, the last
start whose 20 years are complete.

49
00:03:29,877 --> 00:03:34,804
Each time a bill comes due, the money,
interest and all, goes into the next bill

50
00:03:34,804 --> 00:03:36,097
at that month's rate.

51
00:03:36,937 --> 00:03:42,518
Each start is a saver like Dana putting
money in and leaving it alone, and the

52
00:03:42,518 --> 00:03:43,877
results swing hard.

53
00:03:44,877 --> 00:03:49,599
A saver who started in May 1972 ended with

54
00:03:49,599 --> 00:03:54,208
4.612 times the money; one who started in

55
00:03:54,208 --> 00:03:58,817
January 1934 ended with just 1.129 times.

56
00:03:59,857 --> 00:04:04,177
Across all the start months
there are 873 of these rolls.

57
00:04:04,497 --> 00:04:10,802
They overlap a lot, so together they
amount to a few long stretches of history,

58
00:04:10,802 --> 00:04:12,637
not 873 separate tries.

59
00:04:13,777 --> 00:04:19,890
For each one there's a single question:
did the roll end above double? US only,

60
00:04:19,890 --> 00:04:22,057
and history, not a forecast.

61
00:04:23,348 --> 00:04:28,521
Across all of them, the roll ended above
double 52.3 percent of the time, about

62
00:04:28,521 --> 00:04:29,521
half.

63
00:04:29,828 --> 00:04:34,311
But for starts from 1950 to 1989 it was

64
00:04:34,311 --> 00:04:38,450
93.1 percent, nearly always, and for

65
00:04:38,450 --> 00:04:44,428
starts from 1990 on, just
5.0 percent, almost never.

66
00:04:45,548 --> 00:04:52,526
Starts from 1934 to 1949 never got there
at all, so about half is really separate

67
00:04:52,526 --> 00:04:54,508
eras stitched together.

68
00:04:55,988 --> 00:05:00,645
For a saver in Dana's chair, the decade
they happened to start in made almost all

69
00:05:00,645 --> 00:05:01,645
the difference.

70
00:05:02,628 --> 00:05:09,274
Now, the only starts where the promise was
real rather than pretend: the 17 months

71
00:05:09,274 --> 00:05:11,868
from May 2005 to September 2006.

72
00:05:13,108 --> 00:05:17,155
Not one of those 17 rolls reached double;

73
00:05:17,155 --> 00:05:22,188
they ended between 1.378
and 1.388 times the money.

74
00:05:23,068 --> 00:05:27,829
But that is a small sample from a single
era, so it shows what happened once, not

75
00:05:27,829 --> 00:05:29,768
what a guarantee does in general.

76
00:05:31,628 --> 00:05:35,188
What separated those eras was the
path bill rates took after each start.

77
00:05:36,028 --> 00:05:41,470
For starts from 1990 on, the average bill
rate over the following 20 years ended

78
00:05:41,470 --> 00:05:45,688
below the rate in the starting
month 87.6 percent of the time.

79
00:05:46,548 --> 00:05:50,738
When the rate starts high and drifts down,
the average sinks below where it began,

80
00:05:50,738 --> 00:05:54,008
and the roll ends up earning less
than its first bill suggested.

81
00:05:55,048 --> 00:05:58,661
In other words, the rate on the day a
roll begins has been a poor guide to the

82
00:05:58,661 --> 00:05:59,661
decades that follow.

83
00:06:00,228 --> 00:06:03,188
The early eras just ran
the other way more often.

84
00:06:03,988 --> 00:06:08,722
That is not a reason to pick either path;
it is what history did with this pair of

85
00:06:08,722 --> 00:06:09,722
rules.

86
00:06:10,285 --> 00:06:14,385
There's something else double never
promised: what the money will buy.

87
00:06:15,685 --> 00:06:19,765
Over 20 years prices rise,
sometimes slowly and sometimes fast.

88
00:06:20,465 --> 00:06:25,052
To check it, we compared each doubled
amount with how far consumer prices rose

89
00:06:25,052 --> 00:06:26,405
over the same 20 years.

90
00:06:27,325 --> 00:06:31,785
The doubled amount kept its buying
power in 58.7 percent of starts.

91
00:06:32,665 --> 00:06:38,799
The worst start, January 1966, ended with
a double that bought only 58.0 percent of

92
00:06:38,799 --> 00:06:41,385
what the original money had bought.

93
00:06:42,505 --> 00:06:46,951
For Dana's savings, a start like that
would mean twice the dollars buying only a

94
00:06:46,951 --> 00:06:49,785
little more than half of
what the original sum did.

95
00:06:50,925 --> 00:06:56,014
So double is a promise about dollars, not
about groceries, and the bill roll lives

96
00:06:56,014 --> 00:06:59,365
with the same prices, which
this replay doesn't score.

97
00:07:00,427 --> 00:07:02,587
So where would your own 20 years fall?

98
00:07:03,627 --> 00:07:05,507
Here is a line to measure them against.

99
00:07:06,447 --> 00:07:12,807
Averaged over every month since 1934,
the 3-month bill paid 3.42 percent.

100
00:07:12,807 --> 00:07:18,553
For a roll to end above double, the bill
rate has to average about 3.47 percent a

101
00:07:18,553 --> 00:07:23,945
year across all 20 years, which is the
bond's doubling measured the way bill

102
00:07:23,945 --> 00:07:28,627
rates are quoted, so the long-run
average sat just under the line.

103
00:07:29,187 --> 00:07:34,034
The middle roll of all 873 ended at 2.097

104
00:07:34,034 --> 00:07:38,527
times the money, barely over the line.

105
00:07:39,687 --> 00:07:45,122
For money like Dana's, the question
isn't the rate on the day it starts; it's

106
00:07:45,122 --> 00:07:50,558
whether the bill rate averages above
that line over all 80 bills, a rule that

107
00:07:50,558 --> 00:07:54,087
matched the result in every
one of the 873 starts.

108
00:07:55,247 --> 00:07:59,807
Nobody can say where that average will
land, and today's rate can't either.

109
00:08:00,427 --> 00:08:05,912
The line doesn't say which to pick; it
says what the roll has to do to beat the

110
00:08:05,912 --> 00:08:06,912
promise.

111
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:12,562
How the replay was built, and what it
leaves out, like taxes and purchase

112
00:08:12,562 --> 00:08:15,450
limits, is on screen
and in the description.

113
00:08:16,647 --> 00:08:21,223
Back at Dana's table, the two paths look
the same as they did at the start: a

114
00:08:21,223 --> 00:08:24,967
promise of double, or a roll with
no promise past three months.

115
00:08:26,167 --> 00:08:27,647
History doesn't choose for her.

116
00:08:28,147 --> 00:08:33,036
It doesn't know what she needs the money
for, or what else she owns, and it can't

117
00:08:33,036 --> 00:08:34,847
see the 20 years ahead of her.

118
00:08:35,407 --> 00:08:42,004
What it shows is a roll that beat double
about half the time, nearly always for

119
00:08:42,004 --> 00:08:48,435
starts from 1950 to 1989 and almost
never for starts since 1990, with today's

120
00:08:48,435 --> 00:08:52,527
promise imagined for every
start before May 2005.

121
00:08:53,987 --> 00:08:58,367
It shows that double was a promise
about dollars, not about what they buy.

122
00:08:59,007 --> 00:09:03,427
And it shows a line that depends on
a path of rates nobody can see yet.

123
00:09:04,947 --> 00:09:07,667
US only, and history, not a forecast.

124
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:14,714
That's the replay. The next episode,
another question about money and time, is

125
00:09:14,714 --> 00:09:15,755
on screen now.

